# JK Flip-Flop (JK FF) using Verilog HDL

## Overview

This project implements a **positive edge-triggered JK Flip-Flop** with an **active-high reset** using Verilog HDL. A testbench is included to verify the behavior of the flip-flop for all possible input combinations.

## Objective

* Design a positive edge-triggered JK Flip-Flop.
* Implement an active-high reset.
* Verify the flip-flop using a Verilog testbench.
* Observe the output for different combinations of **J** and **K** inputs.

---

## Module Description

### `jk_ff`

The `jk_ff` module changes its output based on the values of **J** and **K** at every positive edge of the clock.

### Inputs

* `clk` – Clock signal
* `rst` – Active-high reset
* `j` – J input
* `k` – K input

### Outputs

* `q` – Main output
* `qb` – Complement of the output (`~q`)

---

## Working

The flip-flop operates on the **positive edge of the clock**.

* If `rst = 1`, the output is reset to `0`.
* Otherwise, the output changes according to the JK truth table.

### JK Logic

| J | K | Operation | Next Q |
| - | - | --------- | ------ |
| 0 | 0 | Hold      | Q      |
| 0 | 1 | Reset     | 0      |
| 1 | 0 | Set       | 1      |
| 1 | 1 | Toggle    | ~Q     |

The complement output is generated using:

```verilog
assign qb = ~q;
```

---

## Testbench Description

### `tb_jk_ff`

The testbench generates a clock signal and applies different combinations of **J** and **K** inputs to verify the flip-flop operation.

### Clock Generation

* Clock period = **10 ns**
* Rising edge every **5 ns**

```verilog
forever #5 clk = ~clk;
```

---

## Test Cases

| Time (ns) | Reset | J | K | Expected Operation |
| --------- | ----- | - | - | ------------------ |
| 0         | 1     | 0 | 0 | Reset (Q = 0)      |
| 10        | 0     | 0 | 0 | Hold               |
| 20        | 0     | 0 | 1 | Reset              |
| 30        | 0     | 1 | 0 | Set                |
| 40        | 0     | 1 | 1 | Toggle             |
| 50        | 0     | 0 | 0 | Hold               |
| 60        | 0     | 0 | 1 | Reset              |
| 70        | 0     | 1 | 0 | Set                |
| 80        | 0     | 1 | 1 | Toggle             |

---

## Simulation Monitoring

The simulation uses the `$monitor` statement to display signal values throughout execution.

```verilog
$monitor("Time=%0t rst=%b j=%b k=%b q=%b qb=%b",
         $time, rst, j, k, q, qb);
```

This helps verify the output at each clock edge.

---

## Expected Behavior

| Reset | J | K | Q (Next State)      |
| ----- | - | - | ------------------- |
| 1     | X | X | 0                   |
| 0     | 0 | 0 | Hold Previous Value |
| 0     | 0 | 1 | 0                   |
| 0     | 1 | 0 | 1                   |
| 0     | 1 | 1 | Toggle              |

---

## Features

* Positive edge-triggered operation
* Active-high reset
* Toggle functionality
* Complement output (`qb`)
* Synthesizable Verilog HDL
* Easy to simulate in Vivado, ModelSim, or other Verilog simulators

---

## Applications

* Counters
* Frequency dividers
* State machines
* Digital control circuits
* Sequential logic design
* FPGA and ASIC implementations

---

## Simulation Result

The simulation verifies that:

* The output resets to `0` when `rst` is HIGH.
* The output follows the standard JK Flip-Flop truth table.
* The output changes only on the **positive edge of the clock**.
* The complement output (`qb`) always remains the inverse of `q`.
